{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Repair verification report\n\nProblem: `rebar config validate` reported tombstones but did not validate known typed values.\n\nProven root cause: `_validate` only called `scan_tombstones`; the canonical `_SECTIONS` coercers were reached only through fail-fast config loading. The real CLI added a second mechanism: pre-dispatch store mounting called `tracker_dir_override()` and raised on `TICKETS_TRACKER_DIR` before validation could aggregate anything.\n\nProof: the held-out real-CLI subprocess combined a tombstoned env input with invalid `reconciler.rich_text_cutover` and `mcp.http_port`. It was RED before repair, and temporary traceback instrumentation localized the early exit to `_main_dispatch -> ensure_store_mounted_best_effort -> tracker_dir_override`. Instrumentation was removed.\n\nTest: `tests/interfaces/contracts/test_tombstone_registry.py::test_config_validate_aggregates_tombstones_and_typed_errors` was held out from the fixer. It is GREEN after repair. Removing the `config validate` no-auto-mount guard reintroduced the exact defect and returned the oracle to RED; the guard was restored. Refactoring litmus passes because the oracle asserts only CLI exit/output.\n\nFix: validation now drives the canonical per-key coercers across user/project/env/CLI layers, aggregates every ConfigError alongside tombstones, preserves unknown/reserved-section policy, and skips store mounting only for `config validate`. Documentation/help describe the expanded contract.\n\nScope and safety: no new dependency; no existing test weakened. Normal `config`, list, and other commands retain auto-mount behavior. Focused config/contracts: 89 passed. `make lint` and `make typecheck` passed.\n\nSiblings/parity: construct coverage is centralized through `_SECTIONS`, so enum, integer, boolean, and other registered coercers share the rule across all four layers. CLI is the validation surface; ordinary library loading intentionally remains fail-fast, and there is no MCP/API validation operation requiring parity.\n\nReplication: the original mixed tombstone + typed-invalid scenario now exits 1 and reports all three diagnostics in one invocation. Residual risk: malformed TOML remains governed by the pre-existing non-aggregation behavior and was not changed in this typed-value bug."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787022114787145001,"uuid":"a77de12e-fbb4-48eb-a3d1-b6fb878f8a95"}